WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.220
Framing is the parser's first structural job.

2
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:08.840
A stream protocol does not necessarily deliver complete application messages in one read.

3
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:15.580
The parser must know how to identify the start and end of each message before it can safely interpret the contents.

4
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:20.500
Datagram protocols preserve message boundaries, but that does not remove validation.

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:26.680
A datagram still has a size, source context, message type, version, and payload.

6
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:34.460
The receiver still needs to decide whether the datagram is long enough, short enough, supported, and valid for the current state.

7
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:42.180
Delimiters, length prefixes, fixed headers, and payload boundaries should be validated before deeper parsing.

8
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.920
A delimiter must be found within a reasonable limit.

9
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:49.500
A length prefix must fit the available input and configured maximums.

10
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.960
A fixed header must be complete before its fields are used.

11
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.400
A complete header does not automatically mean the complete message is available.

12
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:03.100
The header may declare a payload that has not arrived yet, or it may declare a size outside policy.

13
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:09.440
The parser should treat the header as a claim that still needs to be checked against actual bytes and limits.

14
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:15.800
The parser should identify a complete message before dispatching commands or modifying application state.

15
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.300
That ordering prevents partial messages from becoming partial actions.

16
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:27.680
It also keeps protocol behavior predictable when data arrives in pieces, batches, or unusual boundaries.
